 ],
 "literal": "Evvi lost empty-lute bards' / flower-less bare head like, sparse-being / grieve, may you live, O heart! House-tree's / light-possessing wild jasmine smelling / many-dark tresses who is she to us?",
 "translation": "Like the bare, flowerless heads of the bards with empty lutes who have lost their patron Evvi, you have become lacklustre. Grieve then, and may you live, O my heart! Who is she to us anyway—she with the thick, dark tresses that smell of the sun-drenched wild jasmine blooming on our house-tree?",
 "modern": "வள்ளல் எவ்வியை இழந்து வறிய யாழுடன் திரியும் பாணர்களின் பூக்கள் இல்லாத வெற்றுத் தலைகளைப் போலப் பொலிவிழந்து விளங்கும் என் நெஞ்சமே! நீ வாழ்க! நீ வருந்துவாயாக! நம் வீட்டு மரத்தில் படர்ந்து வெயிலில் மலர்ந்த முல்லை மலரின் மணம் கமழும் அடர்ந்த கரிய கூந்தலை உடைய அவள் நமக்கு யார்?",
 "explanation": "The hero speaks to his own heart in a state of yearning and helplessness. He compares his desolate, lacklustre heart to the bare, flowerless heads of bards who have lost their generous patron Evvi and now carry empty lutes. He bids his heart to grieve, questioning what claim they have on the heroine, whose dark, thick tresses smell of the fresh wild jasmine blooming on the tree in their own courtyard. The imagery of the house-tree and the jasmine highlights her domestic presence and his feeling of estrangement. This contrast between intimate proximity and emotional distance perfectly captures the mood of the Marutam landscape.",
